WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:05.370
Electron microscopy has been a gold standard for this kind of ultra 

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:10.140
structure that has really defined how cells are comprised.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:14.980
But it's only recently that we've been able to use 

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:20.240
electron microscopy in a new way to look at 

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.510
three dimensionality of whole cellular 

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:25.320
systems.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.170
The conventional electron microscopy requires you to slice through your 

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:36.480
specimen in order to be able to hit your 

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.400
electrons,

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:42.050
to penetrate deep enough to get an image. So you can't 

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.860
using conventional transmission electron microscopy--

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.850
it's very hard to get a whole cell 

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.050
visualized.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:53.560
In fact,

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:58.550
we really don't know what a whole cell looks like, reconstructed 

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:01.130
at the electron micrograph level.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.050
So there's a technology that is super exciting now that's 

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.550
able to potentially do that,

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.500
and it's called focused ion beam scanning electron microscopy, and the 

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.220
short term for that is FIB-SEM, FIB-- focused ion beam, 

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.050
and SEM-- scanning electron microscopy.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:29.610
So this approach combines the power of 

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:34.330
electron microscopy with an ability to slice through 

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.850
your cell, or your specimen of interest.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:42.490
And the slicing can be done down to four nanometers 

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.250
or less.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:49.610
So you're imaging at four-- you essentially use your scanning electron 

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:54.250
microscope to image through your specimen 

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:59.390
and typically the electrons will go a depth of 

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:00.800
about four nanometers,

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:05.990
and then you just shave off, using your focused ion beam, to mill,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:06.700
it's essentially,

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:11.300
it's just buzzing off the top of that cell that you've just imaged.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.750
And then you do another image four nanometer deep,

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:20.920
and then you use your focused ion beam to essentially saw off that 

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:21.740
portion.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:25.550
And you just do this until you go through the entire cell. 

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.350
At four nanometer slices,

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:33.720
if you slice through a whole cell, that's 40 microns,

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.590
it's a lot of slices.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:41.160
So there are some challenges to putting all of the 

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:45.910
information together. A, each slice you need 

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:49.980
to analyze what you're seeing in each one of those slices,

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.580
and then you have to stitch together all of the 

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:59.310
structures that you have outlined at each slice into one 

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:00.850
huge volume.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:05.450
This is a hugely exciting world in this field,

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.950
because to be able to segment 

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:16.380
structures of interest in each slice requires a lot of either 

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.680
machine learning or painstaking hours for an individual to 

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:25.330
segment a particular structure of interest that you're seeing in that slice.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:28.550
But you got to do that thousands and thousands of times.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.800
So it's pretty much not possible for a human to do this,

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.750
but machines should be able to do it.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:37.850
In fact,

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:42.180
we know that they can do it for simple structures.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:47.300
The challenge is can we essentially engineer 

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:51.900
these computers so that they can pick up some very fine structures, 

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.210
that we can see,

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:57.750
but the computer may have some trouble seeing. If we can do that,

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.640
then we have the possibility to reconstruct all of the 

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.600
substructures within a cell,

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.700
and that would be for the first time.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:12.830
It's a very exciting challenge right now that a lot of 

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:17.600
people are interested in trying to pursue. Now, another aspect 

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:22.280
that makes this particularly challenging is that the microscope 

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:26.250
itself, that is doing the ion beam milling 

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:31.450
and scanning electron microscopy, frequently,

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:33.950
sort of,

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:39.040
it doesn't necessarily work smoothly through thousands and 

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.550
thousands of slices.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:42.180
And so again,

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:47.340
engineers are playing a very important role in making 

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:52.150
sure that that microscope can run continuously for 

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:52.680
weeks,

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:53.880
if not months,

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:59.040
which is what it takes to do an electron 

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:04.180
microscope scan and then a slice with this ion beam. Essentially,

00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:09.280
they have to be in coordination to go for weeks and 

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.720
weeks at a time in order to slice through a 

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:14.350
specimen.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:19.700
So it's another example where lots of different 

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.430
people from different fields-- engineers,

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:22.580
microscopists,

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:30.970
sample preparation-- is all playing an important role in 

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:34.350
allowing this really new 

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:39.280
approach for looking at cells at a very small 

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.950
scale to be possible.

